 "tasks": [
  {
   "id": "01_moving_average",
   "title": "Moving average over a sliding window",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "category": "off-by-one",
   "description": "src/stats.py has a function moving_average(values, k) that should return the mean of every window of k consecutive values, left to right.\n\nFor a list of n values and a window of size k there are n - k + 1 windows, so that is how many averages the function must return. Each average is a float.\n\nThe function must raise ValueError if k is zero or negative, and also if k is larger than the number of values, because no complete window exists in that case. It must not modify the list it is given.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/stats.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.6000000000000001,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "02_slugify",
   "title": "Turn a title into a URL slug",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "category": "strings",
   "description": "src/text.py has a function slugify(text) that should turn any title into a clean URL slug.\n\nThe rules for a slug are:\n- it is lowercase\n- accented letters become their plain ASCII equivalent, so an e-acute becomes e\n- every run of characters that is not a letter or a digit collapses into a single hyphen, so spaces, punctuation and underscores all become one hyphen\n- there is never a hyphen at the start or at the end\n- a title with no letters or digits at all produces the empty string\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/text.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.25,
   "mean_reward": 0.6136363636363635,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "03_cart_defaults",
   "title": "A shopping cart that leaks state between instances",
   "difficulty": "easy",
   "category": "mutable default",
   "description": "src/cart.py has a Cart class with add(name, price, tags), remove(name) and total().\n\nEvery cart must be completely independent: creating two carts and adding an item to one must leave the other empty. Each item must own its own tags list, so appending a tag to one item never changes another item's tags. A cart may also be created from an existing list of items, and in that case changing the caller's list afterwards must not change the cart.\n\nadd stores an item as a dict with the keys name, price and tags. total() returns the sum of the prices. remove(name) deletes the first item with that name and returns True, or returns False if there is no such item.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/cart.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.55,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "04_merge_intervals",
   "title": "Merge overlapping intervals",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "category": "sorting and boundaries",
   "description": "src/intervals.py has a function merge_intervals(intervals) that takes a list of [start, end] pairs and returns the smallest list of pairs that covers exactly the same ground, sorted by start.\n\nThe input may arrive in any order, so the function cannot assume it is sorted. Two intervals that merely touch, such as [1, 2] and [2, 3], cover a continuous range and must come back as a single interval [1, 3]. An interval completely inside another disappears into it. An empty input gives an empty list.\n\nThe function must raise ValueError if any interval has a start greater than its end. It must not modify the list it is given, nor the pairs inside it.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/intervals.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.6666666666666666,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "05_lru_cache",
   "title": "A least-recently-used cache",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "category": "state",
   "description": "src/cache.py has an LRUCache class with put(key, value), get(key, default=None) and len().\n\nThe cache holds at most capacity entries. When a new key arrives and the cache is already full, the entry that was used least recently is the one thrown away.\n\nUsing an entry means touching it in any way that succeeds: a successful get counts as a use, and so does a put that replaces the value of a key already in the cache. Both make that key the most recently used one, so both change which key gets evicted next. A get for a key that is not present returns the default and counts as nothing.\n\nThe constructor must raise ValueError if capacity is zero or negative.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/cache.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.75,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "06_csv_line",
   "title": "Parse one line of CSV",
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "category": "parsing",
   "description": "src/csvline.py has a function parse_csv_line(line) that splits a single line of CSV into a list of field strings.\n\nThe rules are the usual CSV ones:\n- fields are separated by commas\n- a field may be wrapped in double quotes, and inside those quotes a comma is an ordinary character, not a separator\n- inside a quoted field, two double quotes in a row stand for one real double quote in the data\n- the surrounding quotes themselves are not part of the field\n- spaces inside a quoted field are kept exactly as they are\n- an empty field is an empty string, so a line ending in a comma has an empty last field\n- a line with no commas is a single field\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/csvline.py.",
   "max_steps": 15,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.75,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "07_rate_limiter",
   "title": "A sliding-window rate limiter",
   "difficulty": "hard",
   "category": "time and state",
   "description": "src/limiter.py has a RateLimiter class. You build it with RateLimiter(limit, window_seconds) and then call allow(key, now), where key identifies the caller and now is the current time in seconds as a number.\n\nallow returns True if this key is still under its allowance, and records the request. It returns False if the key has already used its allowance, and in that case the request is not recorded.\n\nThe allowance is per key: each key gets its own limit of requests within any window of window_seconds, and one key using up its allowance must have no effect on any other key.\n\nThe window slides and is half-open. A request made exactly window_seconds ago has already left the window and no longer counts against the key, while anything more recent than that still counts.\n\nThe constructor must raise ValueError if limit or window_seconds is zero or negative.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/limiter.py.",
   "max_steps": 20,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.7083333333333334,
   "mean_steps": 4.25
  },
  {
   "id": "08_shortest_path",
   "title": "Cheapest path through a weighted graph",
   "difficulty": "hard",
   "category": "algorithms",
   "description": "src/graph.py has a function shortest_path(graph, start, goal).\n\nThe graph is a dict of dicts: graph[node][neighbour] is the cost of the directed edge from node to neighbour. Costs are numbers and may be zero.\n\nThe function returns a tuple (total_cost, path). The path is the list of nodes from start to goal inclusive, and total_cost is the sum of the edge costs along it. Out of all the routes from start to goal, it must return one of the cheapest, and the cost it reports must match the path it returns.\n\nIf start and goal are the same node the answer is (0, [start]). If the goal cannot be reached, or either node is not in the graph at all, the answer is (inf, []) using math.inf. A negative edge cost must raise ValueError. The graph itself must not be modified.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix src/graph.py.",
   "max_steps": 20,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.75,
   "mean_steps": 4.5
  },
  {
   "id": "09_bank_transfer",
   "title": "Transfer money between accounts without losing any",
   "difficulty": "hard",
   "category": "multi-file, atomicity",
   "description": "This task spans two files. src/accounts.py holds AccountStore, which keeps a balance per account id and offers open, balance, credit and debit, plus the exceptions UnknownAccount and InsufficientFunds. src/ledger.py holds transfer(store, source, destination, amount).\n\nTwo things are required.\n\nFirst, money must be exact. Balances are amounts of currency, so they must be kept as decimal.Decimal values rather than binary floats, and balance() must return a Decimal. Amounts may be handed in as ints, strings or Decimals and must be converted without picking up rounding error, so depositing 0.1 and then 0.2 must leave exactly three tenths.\n\nSecond, a transfer is all or nothing. transfer must raise ValueError if the amount is zero or negative, or if source and destination are the same account; UnknownAccount if either account does not exist; and InsufficientFunds if the source does not hold enough. Whenever a transfer raises, every balance involved must be exactly what it was before the call. A successful transfer moves the amount from source to destination and leaves the total unchanged.\n\nSome of the tests in tests/test_visible.py are failing. Find out why and fix the source files.",
   "max_steps": 20,
   "episodes": 4,
   "solve_rate": 0.5,
   "mean_reward": 0.7083333333333334,
   "mean_steps": 5.25
  }
